Subjective feeling of blocked ears secondary to mild Eustachian tube dysfunction post-coryza
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Aural fullness, Crackling upon swallowing, Retracted tympanic membrane

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-041
Reason for Visit: Acute severe ear pressure and subjective hearing impairment requiring assessment to rule out acute otitis media or sudden sensorineural hearing loss.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden onset of muffled hearing and bilateral aural pressure following a recent cold. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to directly visualize the tympanic membranes, rule out purulent middle ear effusion or tympanic perforation, and confidently exclude sudden sensorineural hearing loss, which requires immediate emergent specialist intervention.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My ears feel completely blocked up and I can barely hear properly; it feels like I'm underwater.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a bilateral sensation of aural fullness and pressure, evolving over the past 48 hours following a resolving upper respiratory tract infection. Reports muffled subjective hearing quality without sudden profound loss. Notes crackling or popping sounds upon swallowing or yawning. Complete absence of severe acute otalgia, fever, vertigo, or purulent aural discharge.
Functional Status: Fully independent, communication requires increased volume.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding potential permanent hearing loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, speaking slightly louder than normal.
Physical Exam: Otoscopic examination reveals bilaterally retracted tympanic membranes lacking an active fluid level or purulence. Intact tympanic membranes without perforation. Diagnostic triad confirmed: aural fullness, crackling upon swallowing, and a retracted tympanic membrane. Normal external auditory canal skin. Lack of localized mastoid tenderness. Gross hearing is symmetric to whisper test; Rinne test is positive bilaterally; Weber does not lateralize.
Environmental Observations: Tissues and nasal decongestants visible in the living area.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking paracetamol with no change in ear pressure.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subjective feeling of blocked ears secondary to mild Eustachian tube dysfunction post-coryza - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of bulging, erythematous tympanic membrane, absence of severe throbbing pain, and no fever.

Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral presentation, clear mechanical etiology (URI), and normal tuning fork test pattern.

Cerumen Impaction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Direct visualization of the tympanic membrane past a clear external auditory canal.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, highly distressing aural fullness and hearing impairment. It was crucial to urgently examine the tympanic membrane to differentiate a benign pressure inequality from an acute suppurative otitis media or an emergent sudden sensorineural hearing loss. Delaying the assessment until regular hours risked irreversible damage if neurosensory loss was present. Clinical confirmation of Eustachian tube dysfunction safely averted an emergency department visit, provided necessary reassurance, and avoided inappropriate antibiotic prescribing.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommendation for regular Valsalva maneuvers (auto-insufflation) and the short-term use of a topical nasal decongestant spray (maximum 3 days) to reduce ostial edema. Oral hydration.
Positive Behavioral Change: Perform the Valsalva maneuver gently every time you take a sip of water, directly linking the mechanical clearing of the tubes to the act of swallowing to ensure regular pressure equalization.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the pressure fails to resolve after 2 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden complete deafness in one ear, severe spinning dizziness (vertigo), or acute weakness in your face.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; persistent blockage requires formal audiometry and tympanometry.
Clinical Course: The muffled sensation will fluctuate but should gradually resolve over 1 to 2 weeks as the post-viral mucosal swelling subsides.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop severe, sharp pain in your ear, if fluid or blood begins draining from the canal, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tube connecting your ear to your throat is swollen from the cold, trapping negative pressure in the middle ear. This pulls your eardrum inward, muffling sound. The nasal spray reduces swelling, and popping your ears forces air back into the space.
Actionable Steps: Use the nasal decongestant spray strictly for only 3 days. Practice gently "popping" your ears frequently. Chew sugar-free gum to promote swallowing.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Blocked Eustachian tubes from a cold. Eardrums are safe, no infection. Use nasal decongestant for 3 days and pop ears gently. Watch for severe pain.

SMS EDUCATION

Chew gum and yawn frequently to help open the tubes in your throat. Do not use decongestant nasal sprays for more than 3 days to avoid rebound swelling.
